# parse-recovery — what the parser does with a file that has mistakes in it.
#
# A tiny task DSL, chosen so every failure mode is one line long:
#
#   task "NAME" due "DATE"   -> a task line, both arguments required
#   done "NAME"              -> a completed task
#   list                     -> a zero-argument statement
#
# `script.capy` is clean and renders. `broken.capy` has two bad lines among
# three good ones, which is the whole point:
#
#   capy run  lib.capy broken.capy   -> refuses, reports the FIRST error, no output
#   capy ast  lib.capy broken.capy   -> returns a tree: 3 statements, 2 error regions
#
# Both behaviours are golden-checked here — `broken.expected-error.txt` holds
# what `run` refuses with, `broken.expected-ast.txt` holds what `ast` recovers.
# See docs/diagnostics.md and docs/ast-json.md.
extension todo

type Date
    pattern "^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$"
end

function task
    arg literal "task"
    arg capture name string
    arg literal "due"
    arg capture when Date
    write `[ ] ${decoded name} (due ${decoded when})
`
end

function done
    arg literal "done"
    arg capture name string
    write `[x] ${decoded name}
`
end

function list
    arg literal "list"
    write `--- end of list ---
`
end
